{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>I will go down now.</b> One needs to know why it says the language of descent, and further why HaShem needs to go down — is not the whole world like a mustard seed before Him? And further, His saying <b>their outcry</b> etc. — does the Master blessed be He lack knowledge? And Rashi, may his memory be for blessing, explained that it comes to teach that a court below may not issue a verdict of judgment except by seeing. But it is difficult according to his words: why was what the verse says in the portion of the Generation of the Dispersion not sufficient for this, where it is written (Genesis 11:5): ״וַיֵּרֶד ה׳״ etc., and we find that Rashi himself explained thus there?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent is that HaShem informed of the order that He conducts with the created beings on the side of kindness and mercy. Now the line of strict justice dictates that everything is according to the one who shames and is ashamed, and when a person transgresses one of all the commandments of HaShem and rebels against the mouth of the Most High, relative to the greatness of the exalted El, he is liable to be destroyed, he and all around him, for he has provoked HaShem, Lord of Hosts the King, and whoever is from the manifestation is liable on account of it. And a person would think that the Master judges His world by this standard, and it is a standard of justice. But if HaShem were to come in judgment in the manner of plaintiff and defendant, standing trial together with the created beings, it is possible they would not be held liable, and even the generation of the Flood might not have been sentenced to death. Therefore HaShem said here, in making known His ways to Abraham, <b>I will go down now</b> — meaning: He will not judge according to the measure of His exaltedness but on an equal footing with the created beings, in the manner the prophet said (Isaiah 43:26): ״נִשָּׁפְטָה יָחַד״ — on an equal footing with the lowlier created beings. And by this measure He will judge and see whether it accords with the measure of the outcry that has come to Me. Meaning: for the one who cries out and demands judgment from HaShem — the demand of justice is calibrated to the measure of the Creator's greatness. Therefore HaShem said: And if, after I calibrate the judgment by the measure of negating My exaltedness, there still remains a measure sufficient for the demand of the attribute of justice to condemn them — complete destruction; and if not — I will know whether they are fit to be judged by this manner or not. From this it follows that the meaning of <b>I will go down now</b> is but a descent within judgment; yet from His place He looks down to know and discerns the hearts of the children of man." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that even though their outcry had come, despite all this He wished to examine them whether at the time of judgment they had not repented and were still holding fast to their wickedness. Therefore He sent the angels in the likeness of men, of beautiful form and appearance, to test them. And this is the meaning of <b>I will go down now</b> — meaning: now I will see whether according to their outcry they have done further destruction, and if not, etc. — and those wicked ones, according to their outcry, proceeded to commit yet another improper act. Therefore immediately and at once the angels reached a verdict and said to Lot (Genesis 19:13): ״מַשְׁחִיתִים אֲנַחְנוּ״ etc. — but beforehand they had said nothing at all. From this you learn: had they not sought to lay hands on the angels, it is possible that HaShem would have granted them an extension of time through the attribute of slow anger. And perhaps HaShem touched upon this the law of the rebellious woman that they said in the Gemara (Shabbat 63a) — that even after she has been admonished for four Sabbaths, at the time of the final verdict they inform her: perhaps you will return. Therefore HaShem sent the angels in the likeness of men, with the intention not to destroy until they observed in them that they were still in their wickedness. And for this reason the verse ״וַיִּפְנוּ מִשָּׁם״ etc. was juxtaposed to ״אֵרְדָה נָּא״ — understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :register {:flags {1 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {2 "The phrase דִּין מוֹרֶדֶת with the citation (דף סג:) refers to the Talmudic passage in Shabbat 63a concerning a defiant/rebellious woman (סוטה מורדת); the page reference confirms the tractate is Shabbat by the Or HaChaim's own citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:25.082018443Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}